.TH mv "" "Command"
.SH NAME
mv \- move/rename files and directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmv [\-f] \fIoldfile newfile\fR
.br
\fBmv [\-f] \fIfile ... directory\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
mv -- move/rename files and directories
Usage:	mv [ -f ] oldfile newfile
	mv [ -f ] file ... directory
Option:	-f	Force; remove current 'newfile', even if unwritable
.HE
.B mv
renames files.
In the first form,
.B mv
changes the name of
.I oldfile
to
.IR newfile .
If
.I newfile
already exists,
.B mv
replaces it with the file being moved; if not,
.B mv
creates it.
If the last argument is a directory,
.B mv
instead uses the second form: each
.I file
is moved so that it resides under that
.IR directory ,
under its own last-component name.
.PP
Normally,
.B mv
performs the move by creating a link from
.I newfile
to
.I oldfile
and then removing
.IR oldfile .
If that fails because
.I oldfile
and
.I newfile
are on different file systems,
.B mv
instead copies
.I oldfile
to
.I newfile
(preserving its permission mode, and, unless the caller owns
.IR oldfile ,
clearing the set-user-id and set-group-id bits) and then removes
.IR oldfile .
.PP
.B mv
will not move a directory across file systems, will not rename
.B .
or
.BR .. ,
and will not move a directory into one of its own descendants.
If
.I newfile
exists, is not a directory, and is unwritable,
.B mv
refuses to remove it unless
.B \-f
is given.
.IP \fB\-f\fR
Force the move: remove an existing, unwritable
.I newfile
instead of refusing to replace it.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cp ,
.B ln
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B mv
prints a message and sets a nonzero exit status for each
.I file
it cannot move, for example because
.I oldfile
does not exist, the caller lacks permission to remove it from its
parent directory, the destination parent directory does not exist or
is not writable, or
.I oldfile
and
.I newfile
are the same file.
.SH NOTES
.B mv
tests the legality of a directory move using search permission on
the directories from the destination back to the root; the superuser
always has search permission and so can construct a move that
disconnects part of the directory tree.
